  next is the one I didn't anticipate, and the function form means that costs you a local three-line function instead of
  an edit to the shared core. The built-in factories keep the common case terse.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should a form declare its validation rules? → Composable functions (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last structural question before I present the design. This one's forced by the repo as it stands: [38;5;153mindex.html[39m loads
  [38;5;153mapp.js[39m with a plain [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag, while [38;5;153msrc/[39m uses CommonJS [38;5;153mrequire[39m. A shared module has to be loadable by both the
  page and a test runner, and today neither mechanism spans both.

  - [1mES modules[0m ([38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, [38;5;153mimport[39m/[38;5;153mexport[39m) — buys native browser loading with no bundler and the same files
    importable by a modern test runner. Costs: [38;5;153mindex.html[39m must be served over HTTP (opening the file directly stops
    working due to module CORS rules), and it diverges from [38;5;153msrc/[39m's CommonJS.
  - [1mGlobal namespace[0m ([38;5;153m<script src="validation.js">[39m setting [38;5;153mwindow.Validation[39m) — buys zero change to how the page loads
    and keeps [38;5;153mfile://[39m working. Costs: a global, and testing in Node needs a shim, which undercuts the testable-core
    decision we just made.
  - [1mCommonJS + a bundler[0m — consistent with [38;5;153msrc/[39m, but adds a build step to a project that currently has none. I'd push
    back on this one; it's the most infrastructure for the least gain here.

  [1mMy recommendation: ES modules.[0m It's the only option that makes the core genuinely testable without extra machinery,
  and needing a local HTTP server is a mild cost for a project already described as a webapp. Flag it if opening
  [38;5;153mindex.html[39m straight off disk matters to you.
